#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""xrpl-trade v0.6: the PROPOSER half of the hardened XRPL skill.

This program NEVER submits a transaction. Write commands (buy/sell/trustline/cancel/send/nft-pin-and-propose/nft-list/nft-send/nft-buy/nft-bid)
Write commands (buy/sell/trustline/cancel/send/nft-pin-and-propose/nft-list/nft-send/nft-buy/nft-bid)
build the
transaction, autofill it against the live network, print the full ceremony
summary (account, network, assets+issuers, amounts, limit price, max spend,
fee, expiry), hash the exact bytes-to-be-approved, and save a proposal.

One deliberate exception: `wallet create` generates a fresh wallet locally
and stores the seed in ~/.xrpl/config.json (0600, atomic) WITHOUT ever
displaying it — the "local signer" deployment profile. The seed is shown
exactly once, and only, by `wallet backup`, for write-down. See "Wallets"
in SKILL.md and the platform boundary in SECURITY.md.

The separate `xrpl-sign` program enforces policy and signs — only after
independent human approval of that exact hash.

Read-only: balance, quote, offers, pairs, inspect-token, plan-trade, reconcile,
nft-inventory, favorites, nft-new, xrpresso (XRPresso marketplace discovery),
giveaway entrants/draw/status/announce (Friday community giveaway).
Local-only: profile (onboarding questionnaire — name, watch-only addresses,
memberships, interests; never leaves this machine), giveaway setup,
nft-stage (artwork validation + hashing before any pin).
Writing always proposes: buy/sell/trustline/cancel/send/nft-pin-and-propose/nft-list/
nft-send/nft-buy/nft-bid, plus giveaway opt-in (proposes the 1-drop entry
payment — approval is still required, and opt-in is only recorded after
the payment is validated on-ledger), plus giveaway gift (proposes a prize
payment from the donation wallet — signed only after the human approves
that exact hash, under the giveaway policy with the donation wallet's key).
The draw only SELECTS a winner — it never builds or sends a gift.
NFT: nft-stage validates + hashes artwork LOCALLY (zero network) and
writes a reviewable stage record; nft-pin-and-propose pins the staged
artwork + metadata to IPFS with the operator's own Pinata key
(bring-your-own-key — the skill never hosts anyone's media), then
proposes the mint. Pinning is an external write, so it lives in the
second, explicitly-run step — never hidden inside a single command.
nft-list proposes an XRP-denominated sell offer.
"""
import argparse
import asyncio
import hashlib
import hmac
